    "result_text": "The full test suite passes (the repo has a single test class, `AcceptanceTest`; the other files in that package are helpers). **BUILD SUCCESS**, 1 test run, 0 failures.\n\n## Summary\n\nOn successful owner creation, the pipeline now emits an audit line via the `AUDIT` logger containing the owner id, customerCode, and registrationDate.\n\n**What I changed:**\n\n1. **New function class** `AuditOwnerCreated.java` (in `rest/function/owner/`) \u2014 a small single-responsibility step that logs to the SLF4J logger named `AUDIT`:\n   ```\n   AUDIT - Owner created: id=11 customerCode=TOW-SUR-0001 registrationDate=2026-09-17\n   ```\n   It reads the persisted `Owner` via `@Val` and emits the three required fields.\n\n2. **Wired it into `owners.POST.yml`** as a new `audit` step between `save` and `respond` (`save \u2192 audit \u2192 respond`), governed by the same `transaction`. It runs after `save` so the generated `id` is populated, and after `assignCustomerCode`/`resolveRegistrationDate` so those fields are set.\n\n**Design rationale:** rather than bolt the logging onto `SaveOwner` (whose job is persistence) or `RespondWithOwnerCreated` (whose job is the HTTP response), I kept auditing as its own step \u2014 matching the project's convention of one small function class per pipeline step with a single responsibility. The `id`/`customerCode`/`registrationDate` are logged exactly as they serialize in the response (`LocalDate.toString()` is ISO format, matching the DTO's `registrationDate`), which is what the `coreLogsAuditOnCreate` test asserts against.",
